Connect rotational predictions to observations and distinguishable parameters (@physics-rotation-observation: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-rotational-accounts-constraints-and-internal-exchange.lingua#r_002C1C42745EA90F6446369066") { r_00BCDFEAE214F01DA32AA888E8
A calculation can reproduce a published model without validating a real apparatus. Use [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../fluid-dynamics/sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6), section 10.4, Example 10.8, as a bounded checkpoint: its negligible-mass support carries six 0.02 kg washers modeled at perpendicular distances 0.25, 0.15 and 0.05 m on each side of the axis. Direct summation gives I=2(0.02)(0.25²+0.15²+0.05²)=0.0035 kg·m². Removing the two nearest washers gives 0.0034 kg·m². At 5 revolutions/s, ω=10π rad/s, so the original kinetic energy is 0.175π² J, approximately 1.73 J. These supplied quantities and approximations reconstruct one inspected published example. The independent tensor implementation sums the individual particle terms and retains the energy coefficient exactly; no physical washer experiment was performed.

The useful inference is conditional. At the same angular speed, the removed pair reduces inertia and energy. If instead removal were modeled with conserved total angular momentum, the removed masses would themselves carry angular momentum and the remaining system would need its own balance. Merely deleting their terms while asserting the same system conservation would change the boundary silently. An actual support mass, washer extent, off-axis mounting or uncertain distance also changes the predicted inertia. Squared distances make geometry matter; the exact rational program does not eliminate physical uncertainty.

The two-rotor model exposes parameter ambiguity. If distinct initial speeds and a final common speed are known, conservation gives I₁/I₂=(ω*−ω₂)/(ω₁−ω*), provided the denominator is nonzero. For positive inertias and distinct speeds, ω* must lie strictly between the initial speeds. Scaling both inertias by a common positive factor leaves ω* unchanged, but scales L and Q*. Thus the observed common speed can identify an inertia ratio under the assumptions, not the absolute inertias or the dissipated energy. If both initial speeds already agree, that observation supplies no ratio information.

An independent known external angular impulse with a measured speed change could identify a fixed axial inertia through I=J_external/Δω, subject to nonzero Δω and a complete external-torque account. Unknown support friction would confound that inference. Similarly a sampled orientation history needs an unwrapping rule or bounds to determine angular speed. Distinct models can fit the same endpoints while predicting different intermediate motion or energy storage; choose an observation that separates their predictions.

For rolling, a measured center acceleration alone does not establish the static-friction inequality. The force/couple example has equal formal accelerations but different required contact forces. Independently known loading, geometry, inertia and normal force are needed to evaluate the candidate. Direct relative-contact motion or a resolved orientation/translation history could distinguish rolling from slip, within the measurement resolution. State an uncertainty interval rather than treating a visually stationary mark as exact proof.

**Work:** reconstruct the published washer sum by hand and compare it with the program. Derive the inertia-ratio formula and its domain, then scale both inertias in the original synchronization example to demonstrate the indistinguishable common speed. Design a paper-based comparison using supplied or published observations: declare measured quantities, model assumptions, competing predictions, uncertainty and a result that would make you revise the model. Reuse shared metrology and inquiry methods at the point of need; no new physical trial or full source reproduction is claimed.

